          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is generalized anxiety disorder, and how is it different from ordinary worry?",
          "answer": "Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD) is persistent, excessive worry about a range of everyday matters. It occurs more days than not for at least six months, is hard to control, and comes with physical and mental symptoms. (Source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder,\" General clinical reference.)\n\nAnxiety itself is normal. It is a useful response to threat. The reference describes GAD as anxiety that is out of proportion to the situation, lasts when circumstances change, and gets in the way of daily functioning.\n\nThe main difference from ordinary worry is the breadth and the impact. The worry moves from topic to topic instead of staying on one situation, and it causes real difficulty in daily life. The symptoms that usually come with it include restlessness, feeling easily tired, trouble concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, and disturbed sleep.\n\nThe retrieved material does not cover causes, treatment, or how a clinician would assess GAD. If you are concerned about your own worry or symptoms, a doctor or other clinician is the right person to talk to.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
